<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_create_graph.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-creategraph">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-creategraph">
  <primary>CREATE GRAPH</primary>
 </indexterm>
 <indexterm zone="cypher-creategraph">
  <primary>ALTER GRAPH</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>CREATE GRAPH</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>CREATE GRAPH</refname>
  <refpurpose>create a graph</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
CREATE GRAPH [ IF NOT EXISTS ] <replaceable class="parameter">graph_name</replaceable> [ AUTHORIZATION <replaceable class="parameter">role_specification</replaceable> ]
CREATE GRAPH ONLY <replaceable class="parameter">graph_name</replaceable>

<phrase>where <replaceable class="parameter">role_specification</replaceable> can be:</phrase>

    <replaceable class="parameter">user_name</replaceable>
  | CURRENT_ROLE
  | CURRENT_USER
  | SESSION_USER
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-creategraph-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   A graph is a schema. <command>CREATE GRAPH</command> creates that schema and puts three
   objects in it: <literal>ag_vertex</literal>, the base vertex label,
   <literal>ag_edge</literal>, the base edge label, and <literal>ag_label_seq</literal>, the
   sequence that hands out label ids.
  </para>

  <para>
   Every label created afterwards inherits from <literal>ag_vertex</literal> or from
   <literal>ag_edge</literal>. That inheritance is what gives an unlabelled pattern such as
   <literal>MATCH (n)</literal> its meaning.
  </para>

  <para>
   The name has to be free as a graph name and as a schema name, because the graph owns a
   schema of that name.
  </para>

  <para>
   If the session has no <varname>graph_path</varname> yet, <command>CREATE GRAPH</command>
   sets it to the new graph. A <varname>graph_path</varname> that is already set is left
   alone.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>CREATE GRAPH ONLY</command> creates the schema and the catalog row and nothing
   else. It is the form <application>pg_dump</application> emits, so that a restore can
   recreate the base labels itself and keep their original label ids. A graph created this
   way cannot hold a label until those base labels exist, and it does not touch
   <varname>graph_path</varname>. The form takes no
   <literal>IF NOT EXISTS</literal> and no <literal>AUTHORIZATION</literal>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-creategraph-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>IF NOT EXISTS</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Issue a notice instead of an error when a graph of that name already exists. Nothing
      is created and nothing existing is changed. This is not a create or replace.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">graph_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Name of the graph to create.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>AUTHORIZATION</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">role_specification</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Role that owns the graph and the objects created with it. Write a role name,
      <literal>CURRENT_ROLE</literal>, <literal>CURRENT_USER</literal> or
      <literal>SESSION_USER</literal>. Without this clause the graph belongs to the role
      that runs the statement.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>ONLY</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Create the schema and the catalog row without <literal>ag_vertex</literal>,
      <literal>ag_edge</literal> or <literal>ag_label_seq</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-creategraph-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples do not use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>. They
   build graphs of their own and drop them at the end.
  </para>

  <para>
   Create a graph:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH boxoffice;

CREATE GRAPH
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   In a session that had no <varname>graph_path</varname> yet, the new graph claims it:
<programlisting>
SHOW graph_path;

 graph_path
------------
 boxoffice
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A second create of the same name reports the graph that is already there and leaves it
   untouched:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH IF NOT EXISTS boxoffice;

NOTICE:  graph "boxoffice" already exists, skipping
CREATE GRAPH
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Give the graph to another role. The role has to exist, so
   <literal>CREATE ROLE curator</literal> runs first:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH festival AUTHORIZATION curator;

CREATE GRAPH
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>ONLY</literal> creates the schema and the catalog row and stops there:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH ONLY skeleton;

CREATE GRAPH
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The graph holds nothing, so a label cannot be created in it. There is no
   <literal>ag_vertex</literal> to inherit from:
<programlisting>
SET graph_path = skeleton;
CREATE VLABEL film;

ERROR:  relation "skeleton.ag_vertex" does not exist
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Drop what the examples built. <literal>skeleton</literal> needs no
   <literal>CASCADE</literal> because nothing was created inside it, and a graph made by the
   plain form always does:
<programlisting>
DROP GRAPH boxoffice CASCADE;
DROP GRAPH festival CASCADE;
DROP GRAPH skeleton;
DROP ROLE curator;
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-creategraph-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   <varname>graph_path</varname> names the graph that unqualified label names belong to. It
   is a session setting, so set it in each new session, or attach it to a database or a role
   with <literal>ALTER DATABASE <replaceable class="parameter">dbname</replaceable> SET
   graph_path = boxoffice</literal>. See <xref linkend="agensgraph-config"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Two more statements act on a graph as a whole, and neither has an
   <literal>IF EXISTS</literal> form:
   <literal>ALTER GRAPH <replaceable class="parameter">graph_name</replaceable> RENAME TO
   <replaceable class="parameter">new_name</replaceable></literal> and
   <literal>ALTER GRAPH <replaceable class="parameter">graph_name</replaceable> OWNER TO
   <replaceable class="parameter">role_specification</replaceable></literal>. A rename moves
   the underlying schema with the graph, and a <varname>graph_path</varname> that named the
   old name follows it.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-creategraph-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 defines the create graph statement in clause 12.4 as
   <literal>CREATE [PROPERTY] GRAPH</literal> with either <literal>ANY</literal> content or a
   graph type that constrains the node types, edge types and properties a graph may hold.
   AgensGraph graphs are always open, so no graph type is written and none can be. The
   <literal>PROPERTY</literal> spelling is not accepted, and neither is the standard's
   <literal>OR REPLACE</literal> form. <literal>AUTHORIZATION</literal> and
   <literal>ONLY</literal> are AgensGraph extensions.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>CREATE GRAPH</command> corresponds to <command>CREATE SCHEMA</command>, and
   <literal>IF NOT EXISTS</literal> and <literal>AUTHORIZATION</literal> mean there what they
   mean here. The contents differ. <command>CREATE SCHEMA</command> creates an empty schema.
   <command>CREATE GRAPH</command> also creates the base labels and the label id sequence,
   and registers the schema in <literal>ag_graph</literal> so that Cypher can find it. A
   plain <command>CREATE SCHEMA</command> does not produce a graph.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

